---
description: Check Python dependencies for missing prebuilt wheels (ARM, Alpine/musl, Windows on ARM, free-threaded Python) with wheelgap, when a build compiles packages from source ("Failed building wheel for", "Microsoft Visual C++ 14.0 is required") or the user asks whether dependencies will install on a platform.
alwaysApply: false
---

- If the `wheelgap` MCP server is available, prefer its tools to the command line: `check_lockfile`, `check_dockerfile`, `check_dist` (a release's `dist/` folder, with `dropped`), `diff_lockfiles`, `check_package`, `explain_install_failure` (a failed install log's text, with the `python` and `targets` it ran on), `suggest_targets` and `list_targets`. From `check_lockfile` and `check_dockerfile`, read `ok` (true only when nothing has a gap) and `gaps` (the packages with one, with `results` and `error`); from `diff_lockfiles`, `worse`, `new` and `ok`; `check_lockfile`'s `passes` is the command line's verdict (it accepts gaps on advisory targets, and source builds with `fail-on = "missing"`); `check_package` takes `explain: true` for `rejected`, and `dependencies: true` to check everything it installs too (`dependency_gaps`, each with `via` and its own `suggest`/`last_with_wheel`; `ok` then covers the whole tree), and for a pinned version returns `suggest` and `last_with_wheel`; `check_lockfile` takes `dependencies: true` for a requirements file, `environment.yml`, `pyproject.toml` or script, and `extras`, `all_extras`, `groups`, `only_groups` and `no_dev` to match a deployment (as `--extra`, `--group`, `--only-group` and `--no-dev` do); statuses, `unknown` and `skipped` mean what they do below. Pass absolute paths or `file://` URIs. The other tools take no selection. `check_lockfile` and `check_dockerfile` take `fix: true` for each gap's way out (`fix` on each entry of `gaps`, `fix_summary`, `gap_wheels`), as `--fix --json` does; `check_dockerfile` takes `inputs: true` (with `context`) for `--dockerfile-inputs`. Setup: add a server running `uvx --from wheelgap wheelgap-mcp` to `.cursor/mcp.json` ([MCP setup guide](https://github.com/wheelgap/wheelgap/blob/main/docs/mcp.md)); if uvx says "An executable named `wheelgap-mcp` is not provided", run `uvx --refresh --from wheelgap wheelgap-mcp --version` once. `uvx wheelgap@latest mcp` starts the same server.
- If `check_dockerfile` with `inputs: true` refuses a line, use the error's line and reason: retry with `path` (a known lockfile or project) and without `inputs` and `context`. That result covers only the explicit input, not what the refused line installs, so don't call the image fine. If it refuses the Dockerfile itself (a `FROM` line or image it can't read, a heredoc Docker rejects), follow the error and use `check_lockfile` with `python` and `targets`. If gaps remain, call again with `fix: true`. Explain that any `gap_wheels` are unofficial builds from PyPI sdists and ask before adding the index. When the list is empty, check `notes` for the reason.
- Run `uvx wheelgap@latest -p <the project's Python> -t <platforms> --json` in the project directory, e.g. `-p 3.13 -t linux-aarch64,musl-aarch64`. Always pass `-p` (without it wheelgap may check free-threaded 3.14t). Or take both from a Dockerfile with `--dockerfile Dockerfile` (plus `--build-arg NAME=VALUE` for any value the build passes; `--dockerfile-inputs` checks the requirements files its final stage installs with `RUN pip install -r FILE`, with `--dockerfile-context DIR` when the build context isn't the Dockerfile's folder; a `RUN` line it can't see into gives `summary.complete: false` and a `dockerfile_warnings` entry, so the answer may be incomplete). For an image wheelgap can't read (`FROM ubuntu:24.04`), pass both `-p` and `-t`. If you don't know the platforms, `uvx wheelgap@latest --suggest-targets --json` reads them from the project's GitHub Actions workflows, `.gitlab-ci.yml` and Dockerfiles (checking nothing); confirm them with the user. `uvx wheelgap@latest --list-targets` lists targets.
- It finds `uv.lock`, `pylock.toml`, `poetry.lock`, `pdm.lock`, `Pipfile.lock`, `requirements.txt`, `requirements.lock` or `pixi.lock`; pass other files by path. With none of those at the root, a directory falls back to `pyproject.toml`, else the one other requirements file, else the one input up to 3 folders down (`directory_inputs` in JSON), and exits 2 listing them when there are several. For `pixi.lock`, it checks one environment's PyPI packages (`--environment NAME`) and skips conda ones. For a library with no lockfile, pass its `pyproject.toml` (direct dependencies only, unless you add `--deps`; `--group NAME` for a `[dependency-groups]` group). A script with a `# /// script` block is read the same way, its `uv lock --script` lock (`script.py.lock`) as a `uv.lock`, and a conda `environment.yml` (by path) for its `pip:` list. To check whether `pip install NAME` works on a platform, pass the name with `--deps` (everything it installs, resolved per target from PyPI's metadata; `--explain` gives the path to each gap in `via`); pin names that are also subcommands, e.g. `mcp==2.3.0`. `--deps` resolves the whole tree of a requirements file or `pyproject.toml` the same way. To check exactly what pip resolved on a machine, pass the report from `python -m pip install --dry-run --ignore-installed --report report.json …` made there, with that machine's `-p` and `-t`. For a failed pip, uv, Poetry or `pdm install -v` install, `--from-log install.log -p <Python> -t <platform>` finds the packages that failed to build and says why no wheel fits and which release has one.
- If the user names a deployment distro, pass `--distro NAME` (e.g. `ubuntu-22.04`, `alpine`; `--list-targets` lists them), or for one that isn't listed, verify its libc version and pass `--glibc X.Y` or `--musl X.Y`. None of these select platforms, so Alpine still needs a `musl-*` target.
- Private indexes: packages a lockfile pins to another index are never looked up on PyPI: they keep their locked files, or are skipped as `private index` when the lock lists none, and `--index-url` doesn't change that. `--index-url URL` sends the lookups for requirements files, `pyproject.toml` and package names to that index (PEP 691 JSON), and `--offline` never contacts an index. wheelgap ignores the project's own index settings (an `--index-url` line in a requirements file, `[tool.uv]` indexes, `pip.conf`, `PIP_INDEX_URL`), so in a project that installs from a private index pass `--index-url` or check the lockfile, or those names go to pypi.org ([private indexes](https://github.com/wheelgap/wheelgap/blob/main/docs/how-it-works.md#private-indexes)).
- Read `summary.with_gaps` and each package's `results` (target label from `targets[].label` → `wheel`, `source`, `missing`, `unknown`, `n/a` or `bundled`), not just the exit code (`0`/`1` gaps/`2` bad input). `skipped` lists packages that weren't checked (editable, git, path, URL, private index). A `uv.lock` is answered as `uv sync` installs it; with `--online`, `pip_refuses` lists the files pip would refuse (a `Requires-Python` upper bound uv ignores).
- Treat `unknown` as a failed lookup (see the package's `error`), not as fine. `--explain` adds why each wheel was rejected (`rejected`) and, for `uv.lock`, which dependency pulls it in (`via`), plus a link to the package's issue tracker when PyPI has one (`ask for a wheel: <url>`, `issues_url` in JSON); the text table names a transitive gap's direct dependency, e.g. `cryptography (via mcp)`; `--explain --notes` adds the coverage tracker's workarounds (`notes`), sending gap package names to wheelgap.github.io.
- Suggest fixes, starting with `--fix` (`--fix --json`): each package's `fix` maps its gap targets to `kind` `upgrade` (`version` and `how`, e.g. `uv lock --upgrade-package NAME` or the new pin; `constraint.allows: false` means the user's own requirement needs relaxing first), `gap_wheel` (no newer release has the wheel, but an unofficial one on wheelgap's gap wheel index does: `wheel`, `url`, `sha256`) or `none` with a `reason`; `fix_summary` counts them. wheelgap doesn't resolve, so re-lock and re-run after an upgrade. Gap wheels are built by wheelgap from the PyPI sdist, not by the maintainers: say so and ask before adding the index (`[[tool.uv.index]]`/`[tool.uv.sources]` or `--extra-index-url`, as the output gives; `--fix --print-gap-wheel-snippet` prints just those) to project files. For a uv.lock, follow the output's note on a package that isn't a direct dependency (uv applies `[tool.uv.sources]` to direct dependencies only); for other lockfiles it only says to add the index where the lock is made and relock, so never invent `[tool.uv]` or `--extra-index-url` lines for them (a pdm.lock, and a poetry.lock package that records no index, get no gap wheel: wheelgap can't tell they're from PyPI); never suggest the index in a project that installs from a private index. `--fix` sends gap package names to pypi.org and reads the gap wheel list from wheelgap.github.io, is off with `--index-url` and refused with `--offline`, and never changes the exit code. wheelgap doesn't read the project's index settings, so for a requirements file, `pyproject.toml`, an `environment.yml`'s `pip:` list, package names or a script, a re-run after adding the index still reports the gap: don't loop on it. An empty `gap_wheels` isn't proof there's no gap wheel: it's also `[]` when the list couldn't be read or the run uses another index. Otherwise pin a version that has the wheel (`--explain --suggest` names the nearest newer one, `suggest` in JSON, or the last older one when no newer one has it, `last_with_wheel`; on by default with `--deps --explain`, off with `--no-suggest`; `--write-constraints FILE` writes those pins as a pip/uv constraints file to check with `pip install -c FILE …`), use a `-slim` base instead of Alpine, or add build dependencies deliberately (`--explain --build-info` says what a source build needs; it downloads sdists). Accept a known gap with `--ignore NAME`, or `--ignore NAME:TARGET` for one platform (or `ignore = [...]` in `[tool.wheelgap]`), never by hiding it. To watch a platform without failing on it, add it to `-t` and pass `--advisory TARGET`.
- A `uv.lock` made with `[tool.uv] environments` covers only those: a target outside them is an error (exit 2), not a gap.
- By default, `uv.lock`, `poetry.lock` and `pdm.lock` checks conservatively include extras and development groups. Match the deployment with `--no-dev`, `--group NAME` or `--only-group NAME` (and `--extra NAME` for uv.lock and Poetry 2.x poetry.lock) before treating a gap as a deployment failure.
- After a dependency upgrade, `uvx wheelgap@latest diff OLD NEW -p … -t … --json` lists what got worse; `OLD` can be a git ref such as `origin/main:uv.lock` (path relative to the repo root). For a `-p`, `-t` or libc floor you don't pass, `diff` reads `[tool.wheelgap]` and `.python-version` from the `NEW` lockfile's project (the MCP `diff_lockfiles` tool doesn't); `diff NAME==OLD NAME==NEW --deps` compares two releases of a package. The GitHub Action's `compare-to: base` runs this on pull requests.
- Before a release, `uvx wheelgap@latest --dist dist -p … -t …` checks the wheels and sdist about to be uploaded and fails when a target the previous PyPI release had a wheel for has none (`dropped` in JSON); the Action's `dist: dist` input does the same. `--badge PATH` writes a shields.io endpoint file for a README badge.
- For new-Python readiness, check `-p <current>,next` (or `next-t`): `blocking_newer_python` lists packages with a gap only on the newer one.
- For a `linux-armv7l` gap (32-bit Raspberry Pi OS), `--explain --piwheels` says when piwheels has a wheel of that version, with the `--extra-index-url` line to install it.
- wheelgap reports wheel availability only, not free-threading safety.
